--- a/docs/user/drivers.md
+++ b/docs/user/drivers.md
@@ -64,6 +64,9 @@ Cisco IOS is hier_config's primary reference platform and the most thoroughly te
 - **[Sectional exiting](glossary.md#sectional-exiting)**: BGP `peer-policy` and `peer-session` blocks require `exit-peer-policy` and `exit-peer-session` closure tokens.
 - **Per-line substitutions**: strips `Building configuration…` banners and timestamp headers.
 - **VLAN id list splitting**: IOS can render unnamed VLANs collapsed onto a single comma/range line (e.g. `vlan 69,381`, `vlan 10-12`), depending on how the VLANs were created — named VLANs always get their own block, and the grouping shifts as VLANs are named or unnamed. When such a collapsed line is present, a post-load callback splits it into one `vlan <id>` block each so the VLANs diff block-to-block against an intended config that lists them separately — avoiding a destructive `no vlan 69,381`.
+- **Trunk VLAN delta remediation**: a long `switchport trunk allowed vlan` list wraps across multiple running-config lines on IOS — a base line plus one or more `add <ids>` continuation lines. A post-load callback merges these into one canonical line first, then a post-remediation callback rewrites a full negate-and-reapply pair into the incremental `switchport trunk allowed vlan add <ids>` / `remove <ids>` deltas IOS actually supports, matching how operators adjust trunk VLANs day-to-day.
+
+  **Known limitation**: `future()` and rollback don't fold these `add`/`remove` deltas back into the interface's canonical VLAN list — the predicted future config keeps them as separate literal lines alongside the original list rather than merging them (see [Future Config: Known Limitations](future-config.md#known-limitations)).
 
